# Canonical citation metadata for the Collaboration Station (Research) framework.
# GitHub reads this file to render the "Cite this repository" button.
#
# ── IF YOU ARE USING THIS REPOSITORY AS A TEMPLATE FOR YOUR OWN PROJECT ──
# Replace `title`, `abstract`, `url`, `repository-code`, `date-released`, and
# `authors` with your project's own values. As shipped, this file describes the
# framework itself, so a project that leaves it unchanged will claim to be
# Collaboration Station. Add `version: "x.y.z"` when you cut a release, and omit
# any release field you cannot yet fill rather than leaving a placeholder —
# GitHub and other tools parse this file, so it must stay valid when it lands.
# Set `type` to `dataset` if the primary released output is data rather than
# software.
#
# Authorship note: the citable author is the human director, with the
# organization as the affiliation. AI tools are not listed as formal authors
# under current citation norms (they cannot take responsibility for the work),
# so the AI collaboration is disclosed in `abstract` instead. This is deliberate
# and matches Dandelion Engineering's transparency standard.
#
# License scope is intentionally NOT declared here: this work is path-scoped
# (MIT for code, CC BY 4.0 for prose, datasets under their source licenses), and
# CFF treats multiple `license:` values as "either/or," which would misstate that
# split. `LICENSING.md` is the authoritative license-scope record.
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use Collaboration Station, or adapt it for your own work, please cite it using the metadata below."
title: "Collaboration Station (Research): a plain-language framework for running exploratory scientific research projects with AI agents"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Crespo
    given-names: Randy
    affiliation: Dandelion Engineering
abstract: >-
  Collaboration Station is a plain-language framework for running exploratory
  scientific research projects with AI agents. Its core is a folder structure
  and instructions in ordinary English, with small standard-library Python
  utilities for collision-safe chat checkpoints and path-scoped Git closeout;
  it requires no third-party package installation or bundled credentials. The
  framework lets agents carry a research project across sessions with continuity
  handled by files, disjoint active-work claims for safe parallel writing, and
  exact-state Review Cards for cross-review. Projects are bound by a fifteen-slot
  Claim Sheet that predeclares success, failure, and inconclusive outcomes before
  results exist; changes run through an append-only amendment protocol; and each
  project concludes with a technical report, a plain-language piece, a
  self-contained reproducibility packet, and a director-facing study guide. The
  framework is aimed at the stage before a researcher commits significant time
  or funding to an idea and is designed to make a clean negative result cheap to
  reach. It was developed by Randy Crespo (Dandelion Engineering) in
  collaboration with the AI research agents Claude (Anthropic) and Codex
  (OpenAI); the AI contribution is disclosed here per Dandelion Engineering's
  transparency standard.
url: "https://github.com/Dandelion-Engineering/Collaboration-Station-Research"
repository-code: "https://github.com/Dandelion-Engineering/Collaboration-Station-Research"
date-released: 2026-07-31
keywords:
  - AI agents
  - multi-agent collaboration
  - exploratory research
  - research methodology
  - reproducibility
  - open science
